  ('3448c4b0-d167-5637-9d4f-e523c54e80be', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'math-g10-transformations'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Which rule describes a transformation that preserves the distance between every pair of points?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$(x, y) \\to (x + y, y)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-shear-is-rigid"},{"text":"$(x, y) \\to (2x, 2y)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-dilation-is-rigid"},{"text":"$(x, y) \\to (x, 3y)$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-stretch-is-rigid"},{"text":"$(x, y) \\to (y + 3, x - 1)$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$(x, y) \to (y + 3, x - 1)$$q$, $q$The rule $(x, y) \to (y + 3, x - 1)$ is a reflection over $y = x$ followed by a translation, and both are rigid motions, so distances stay the same. The choice $(x, y) \to (2x, 2y)$ is a dilation that doubles every distance, so the segment from $(0, 0)$ to $(1, 0)$ becomes length 2.$q$, 'draft', null)
